note draft
# `mine`/`form`/`any`/`range`/`send` are the feed grammar DSL's own vocabulary (note/term/feed/01-grammar.md) and
# not Term identifiers, so this file is not compiled AS code. It is compiled FROM: `term make` recognises a
# grammar, generates the reader it describes and compiles that (deck/call/code/make.ts, held by
# test/compile/feed-mill-build.ts). What passes the type checker is the generated reader, not this.
#
# Still shelved with `note draft` only because ./code.tree still hand-writes `read-hex`, and two definitions of one
# name in a package collide. Removing the marker is the hex half of format-mill-0007, and it goes together with
# deleting that task from ./code.tree. See note/term/feed/05-parser-and-mill.md.
#
# `send <name>` emits a match as a named field on the enclosing rule. `take`/`bind` is reserved for parameters a
# rule receives from its caller (mine int's width/order/sign, an offset passed down for mine at) — hex needs none.
#
# `mine value` (new): the rule's own result, an ordinary Term expression over its `send`-named captures — the
# grammar describes SHAPE (sequence, alternation, repetition), value computation stays ordinary Term (real
# helpers like `hex-digit-value`, not arithmetic reinvented as grammar syntax). Without one, a rule's value is
# whatever it captured with no further combination needed — true for `hex` (a `mine list`'s own value is already
# the list of its matches) but not for `hex-digit`/`hex-byte`, which combine more than one capture into one
# scalar. Found missing, not assumed: `note/term/project/feed-compiler.md`'s first real compile attempt (`hex`,
# the simplest dialect) discovered the original version of this file had no way to express "combine two nibble
# captures into a byte" at all — every earlier session that hand-wrote ./code.tree's `read-hex` did that
# combination in the reader's own logic, invisibly, without the grammar ever having to say it.

# a `mine value` is an ordinary Term expression and may call a real helper, so the grammar imports what it calls
# the way any other .tree does. `feedMineLoads` carries these into the generated reader verbatim.
load ./code
  find hex-digit-value

mine hex
  mine list
    mine form, form hex-byte

mine hex-byte
  mine form, form hex-digit
    send high
  mine form, form hex-digit
    send low
  mine value
    call add
      call multiply
        read high
        code 16
      read low

mine hex-digit
  mine any
    mine range
      bind base, text <0>
      bind head, text <9>
    mine range
      bind base, text <a>
      bind head, text <f>
    mine range
      bind base, text <A>
      bind head, text <F>
  send char
  mine value
    call hex-digit-value
      call char-code-at
        read char
        code 0
